  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSatellite and water vapor loops this afternoon show a mid/upper\nclosed low over the Upper Midwest, with a surface low stacked\nbeneath over northern Wisconsin. This is placing an occluded\ncold front from Lower Michigan through western Ohio. The front\nbeing so far displaced from the closed low, as well as no true\ncold air source, is leading to limited dynamics with this\nsystem. However, a 40-50 knot southwesterly low-level jet is\nhelping to draw a band of deep moisture into the region to\nsupport fairly steady rain ahead of the frontal boundary. This\nrain will continue to be mainly light given the weak dynamics,\nbut expect it to be persistent through the evening. There should\nbe a lull after 00Z as the deepest moisture plume shifts east,\nbut expect one more uptick in showers right along the frontal\nboundary in the 02-07Z period, especially in NE Ohio and NW PA.\nThis has been a consistent signal in RRFS runs. Total QPF\nthrough tonight still looks to average 0.25 to 0.50 inch, so no\nimpacts are expected other than beneficial rain after being dry\nfor almost a full month. \n\nAs the mid/upper low lifts through the northern Great Lakes\ntonight and into northern Ontario Thursday, a dry slot will wrap\ninto the region, so expect dry conditions late tonight through\nThursday. This will allow for quite a bit of sunshine Thursday\nand continued mild temperatures, but it will be breezy due to\nthe relatively tight pressure gradient on the southern edge of \nthe mid/upper low. Expect SW winds to gust to 20-30 knots at\ntimes from late morning through the afternoon as sunshine and\nthe dry profile leads to deep mixing.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA stronger mid/upper trough and associated closed low remain on\npace to dive through the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes Thursday\nnight and Friday. This will push a stronger cold front through\nthe region Friday, leading to a more substantial airmass change\nto seasonable temperatures Friday through the weekend (highs in\nthe low/mid 50s). Moisture return ahead of the front is brief\nbefore the front pinches it off, so even though showers will\ngradually push into the region from west to east Thursday night\nand Friday morning, QPF will be very light. Coverage of showers\nFriday will be highest in NE Ohio and NW PA where boundary layer\nwinds shifting to W then NW behind the front and 850 mb temps\ndropping to 0 to -3 C will support lake enhancement, so this\nwill keep showers going through the day in those areas. A\ntransition to pure lake effect rain showers Friday night will\ncontinue through at least Saturday morning as NW flow, cold air\nadvection, and wraparound boundary layer moisture continues.\nThis will keep showers going across the \"snowbelt\" areas of NE\nOhio and NW PA, especially eastern Cuyahoga, Lake, Geauga, \nAshtabula, Trumbull, Erie, and Crawford Counties. Drier air and\na lowering inversion will finally end the lake effect by\nSaturday night as high pressure noses in from the Mississippi\nValley, setting up a dry but seasonably cool Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nAnother cold front will drop through the region Sunday\nnight/Monday tied to a mid/upper trough diving through the\neastern Great Lakes. This could bring a few lake enhanced\nshowers to NE Ohio and NW PA, but very low-impact since the best\nforcing will pass to the east. Despite this front grazing the\narea, temperatures will actually moderate early next week as a\ncontinued split mid/upper flow across the CONUS floods Pacific-\nbased air eastward, so expect highs in the upper 50s to low 60s\nMonday through Wednesday. A stronger trough could possibly bring\ncooler air the second half of the week, but there continues to\nbe no source region for arctic air anytime soon as most of\nCanada remains under abnormally high mid/upper heights and mild\nPacific air.",
